("h2", "2. Prose Summary in Order"),
("ol", [
 "**Setting.** At about twelve, the narrator stayed in a village in north Karnataka with her grandparents. Transport was poor, so the morning paper came in the afternoon and the weekly magazine one day late. Everyone waited eagerly for the bus.",
 "**The novel.** Triveni was a popular Kannada writer who died young. Her novel *Kashi Yatre* appeared as a serial in the weekly *Karmaveera*. It tells of an old lady who longs to go to Kashi but gives all her savings for the wedding of a young orphan girl, saying the girl's happiness is more important than worship at Kashi.",
 "**Avva and the serial.** The grandmother, Krishtakka, had never gone to school and could not read. Every Wednesday the narrator read the next episode to her. Avva listened with great concentration, could repeat the text by heart and identified with the old lady, because she too never went to Kashi. Afterwards she discussed the episode with her friends in the temple courtyard.",
 "**The tears.** The narrator went to a wedding in a neighbouring village and stayed a week instead of a couple of days. On her return she found Avva in tears, which she had never seen before.",
 "**The night talk.** On the open terrace under a full moon Avva told her story: she lost her mother young, her father remarried, girls were not thought to need education, she married young and became busy with children and grandchildren. Now she could not read the new episode, felt embarrassed to ask others, and felt dependent and helpless.",
 "**The decision.** Avva decided to learn the Kannada alphabet from next day and to read a novel on her own by Saraswati Puja day during Dassara. The narrator laughed childishly at her age; Avva replied that for a good cause a determined person can overcome any obstacle and for learning there is no age bar.",
 "**The lessons.** The narrator became her teacher and Avva a wonderful student who read, repeated, wrote and recited, doing amazing amounts of homework.",
 "**The festival.** At Dassara the narrator secretly bought *Kashi Yatre*, now published as a novel. Avva gave her frock material and touched her feet, saying she was respecting a teacher, not a granddaughter. The narrator returned the namaskara and gave her gift. Avva read the title and the publisher's name at once, so the narrator knew her student had passed with flying colours."]),

